  "worked_solution": "First, notice that a matrix of rank $4$ must have at least $4$ columns and at least $4$ rows. \nSo, we disregard the following matrices:\n\n- $A$ since it has only $3$ columns (and only $3$ rows).\n\n- $C$ since it has only $3$ columns.\n\n- $D$ since it has only $3$ rows.\n\nAlso, the matrix must have $4$ pivot columns. As we can see, the matrix $E$ has exactly $4$ pivot columns, while the matrix $B$ has only $2$ pivot columns. So, $\\text{rank}(E)=4$ and $\\text{rank}(B)=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the matrix $E.$",
